.TH "vsearch-fastq_filter" "1" "April 29, 2026" "version 2.31.0" "vsearch manual"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
vsearch --fastq_filter \[em] trim and filter fastq sequences
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[B]vsearch\f[R] \f[B]--fastq_filter\f[R] \f[I]fastqfile\f[R]
[\f[B]--reverse\f[R] \f[I]fastqfile\f[R]] (\f[B]--fastaout\f[R] |
\f[B]--fastaout_discarded\f[R] | \f[B]--fastaout_discarded_rev\f[R] |
\f[B]--fastaout_rev\f[R] | \f[B]--fastqout\f[R] |
\f[B]--fastqout_discarded\f[R] | \f[B]--fastqout_discarded_rev\f[R] |
\f[B]--fastqout_rev\f[R]) \f[I]filename\f[R] [\f[I]options\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The vsearch command \f[V]--fastq_filter\f[R] trims and filters the
sequences in a fastq file (see \f[V]vsearch-fastq(5)\f[R]).
It is similar to \f[V]--fastx_filter\f[R] (see
\f[V]vsearch-fastx_filter(1)\f[R]), but restricted to fastq input only.
.PP
Sequences are first \f[B]trimmed\f[R], then \f[B]filtered\f[R] based on
the remaining bases:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Trimming\f[R] shortens sequences using \f[V]--fastq_stripleft\f[R],
\f[V]--fastq_stripright\f[R], \f[V]--fastq_truncee\f[R],
\f[V]--fastq_truncee_rate\f[R], \f[V]--fastq_trunclen\f[R],
\f[V]--fastq_trunclen_keep\f[R], and \f[V]--fastq_truncqual\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Filtering\f[R] discards sequences that do not satisfy criteria set
by \f[V]--fastq_maxee\f[R], \f[V]--fastq_maxee_rate\f[R],
\f[V]--fastq_maxlen\f[R], \f[V]--fastq_maxns\f[R],
\f[V]--fastq_minlen\f[R] (default 1), \f[V]--fastq_minqual\f[R],
\f[V]--fastq_trunclen\f[R], \f[V]--maxsize\f[R], and
\f[V]--minsize\f[R].
.PP
If no trimming or filtering options are given, all sequences are written
to the output files, possibly after conversion to fasta format.
.PP
For paired-end reads, the file with reverse reads is specified with
\f[V]--reverse\f[R], and the corresponding outputs are written to
\f[V]--fastaout_rev\f[R], \f[V]--fastqout_rev\f[R],
\f[V]--fastaout_discarded_rev\f[R], and
\f[V]--fastqout_discarded_rev\f[R].
Both reads of a pair must pass all filters for either to be retained; if
one fails, both are discarded.
.PP
Discarded sequences are written to the \f[V]*_discarded\f[R] files in
their trimmed form: truncation (e.g.\ \f[V]--fastq_truncqual\f[R]) is
applied before the filters, so a read truncated and then rejected
appears truncated in the discarded output.
With \f[V]--reverse\f[R], the kept/discarded counts in the final report
refer to pairs, not to individual reads.
.PP
After processing, vsearch reports the number of sequences kept and
discarded, and how many of the kept sequences were trimmed.
Use \f[V]--eeout\f[R] or \f[V]--fastq_eeout\f[R] to annotate output
headers with the expected error count.
See \f[V]vsearch-expected_error(7)\f[R] for details on expected errors.
.PP
To illustrate the effect of
\f[V]--fastq_trunclen 6 --fastq_maxee 1.0\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
Input (3 reads):   --fastq_trunclen 6 --fastq_maxee 1.0:

\[at]r1                kept (--fastqout):
ACGTACGTAC         \[at]r1 (truncated to ACGTAC; expected error < 1.0)
\[at]r2         -->
ACGT               discarded (--fastqout_discarded):
\[at]r3                \[at]r2 (4 bases < 6, discarded by --fastq_trunclen)
NNNNNNNNNN         \[at]r3 (expected error > 1.0 after truncation)
\f[R]
.fi
.SH OPTIONS
.SS mandatory options
.PP
At least one output option must be specified: \f[V]--fastaout\f[R],
\f[V]--fastaout_discarded\f[R], \f[V]--fastaout_discarded_rev\f[R],
\f[V]--fastaout_rev\f[R], \f[V]--fastqout\f[R],
\f[V]--fastqout_discarded\f[R], \f[V]--fastqout_discarded_rev\f[R], or
\f[V]--fastqout_rev\f[R].
.SS core options
.SS trimming
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_stripleft\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Remove the specified number of bases from the 5\[cq] (left) end of each
read.
Reads reduced to zero length are discarded.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_stripright\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Remove the specified number of bases from the 3\[cq] (right) end of each
read.
Reads reduced to zero length are discarded.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_truncee\f[R] \f[I]non-negative real\f[R]
Truncate reads so that their cumulative expected error does not exceed
\f[I]real\f[R].
Truncation occurs at the first position where the threshold would be
exceeded.
Negative arguments are rejected.
See \f[V]vsearch-expected_error(7)\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_truncee_rate\f[R] \f[I]non-negative real\f[R]
Truncate reads so that their average expected error per base does not
exceed \f[I]real\f[R].
Truncation occurs at the first position where the threshold would be
exceeded.
The average expected error per base is the total expected error divided
by the length of the truncated sequence.
Negative arguments are rejected.
See \f[V]vsearch-expected_error(7)\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_trunclen\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Truncate reads to \f[I]positive integer\f[R] bases.
Reads shorter than \f[I]positive integer\f[R] bases are discarded.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_trunclen_keep\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Truncate reads to \f[I]positive integer\f[R] bases.
Reads shorter than \f[I]positive integer\f[R] bases are kept.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_truncqual\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Truncate reads starting at the first base whose quality score is at or
below \f[I]positive integer\f[R].
Accepted values range from 0 to 93.
.SS filtering
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_maxee\f[R] \f[I]positive real\f[R]
Discard sequences with an expected error greater than \f[I]real\f[R].
The expected error is the sum of error probabilities for all positions
in the sequence, and is strictly positive (zero or negative arguments
are rejected, as they would discard every sequence).
Applied after trimming.
See \f[V]vsearch-expected_error(7)\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_maxee_rate\f[R] \f[I]non-negative real\f[R]
Discard sequences with an average expected error per base greater than
\f[I]real\f[R].
The average expected error per base is the total expected error divided
by the sequence length, and is always in the [0.0, 1.0] range; negative
arguments are rejected, and values greater than 1.0 effectively disable
the filter.
Applied after trimming.
See \f[V]vsearch-expected_error(7)\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_maxlen\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Discard sequences longer than \f[I]positive integer\f[R] bases.
Applied after trimming.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_maxns\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Discard sequences containing more than \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
ambiguous bases (N).
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_minlen\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Discard sequences shorter than \f[I]positive integer\f[R] bases.
Applied after trimming.
Default is 1.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_minqual\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Discard sequences containing any base with a quality score below
\f[I]positive integer\f[R].
Default is 0 (no filtering).
.TP
\f[V]--maxsize\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Discard sequences with an abundance greater than \f[I]positive
integer\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--minsize\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Discard sequences with an abundance smaller than \f[I]positive
integer\f[R].
.SS output
.TP
\f[V]--eeout\f[R]
Add the expected error count to each sequence header in output fasta or
fastq files, as the annotation \f[V];ee=float\f[R].
Synonym of \f[V]--fastq_eeout\f[R].
Use \f[V]--xee\f[R] to remove this annotation from headers.
See \f[V]vsearch-expected_error(7)\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_eeout\f[R]
Add the expected error count to each sequence header in output fasta or
fastq files, as the annotation \f[V];ee=float\f[R].
Synonym of \f[V]--eeout\f[R].
Use \f[V]--xee\f[R] to remove this annotation from headers.
See \f[V]vsearch-expected_error(7)\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--fastaout\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write sequences passing the filter to \f[I]filename\f[R], in fasta
format.
When \f[V]--reverse\f[R] is used, only forward reads are written here.
.TP
\f[V]--fastaout_discarded\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write the sequences not selected to \f[I]filename\f[R], in fasta format.
.TP
\f[V]--fastaout_discarded_rev\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write the reverse reads not passing the filter to \f[I]filename\f[R], in
fasta format.
Requires \f[V]--reverse\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--fastaout_rev\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write the reverse reads passing the filter to \f[I]filename\f[R], in
fasta format.
Requires \f[V]--reverse\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--fastqout\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write sequences passing the filter to \f[I]filename\f[R], in fastq
format (see \f[V]vsearch-fastq(5)\f[R]).
Requires fastq input.
When \f[V]--reverse\f[R] is used, only forward reads are written here.
.TP
\f[V]--fastqout_discarded\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write the sequences not selected to \f[I]filename\f[R], in fastq format.
Requires input in fastq format.
.TP
\f[V]--fastqout_discarded_rev\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write the reverse reads not passing the filter to \f[I]filename\f[R], in
fastq format.
Requires \f[V]--reverse\f[R] and fastq input.
.TP
\f[V]--fastqout_rev\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write the reverse reads passing the filter to \f[I]filename\f[R], in
fastq format.
Requires \f[V]--reverse\f[R] and fastq input.
.TP
\f[V]--reverse\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Specify the FASTQ file containing the reverse reads.
.TP
\f[V]--sizein\f[R]
Use the abundance annotations present in sequence headers when reading
fasta or fastq file.
Search for the pattern \f[V][>\[at];]size=integer[;]\f[R].
Entries without abundance annotations are silently assumed to be of
\f[V]size=1\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--sizeout\f[R]
Add abundance annotations to sequence headers when writing fasta or
fastq files.
Add the pattern \f[V];size=integer\f[R].
If option \f[V]--sizein\f[R] is not used, abundance values are set to 1
for all entries.
If \f[V]--sizein\f[R] is used, existing abundance annotations are simply
reported to output files.
.SS secondary options
.TP
\f[V]--bzip2_decompress\f[R]
Specify that the \f[I]input pipe\f[R] is streaming data compressed using
Huffman coding.
See \f[V]bzip2(1)\f[R] for more details.
This option is required when compressed data arrives on standard input
through a pipe (`-'), where the format cannot be detected without
consuming the stream.
It is not needed when reading from a regular file compressed with bzip2,
nor when such a file is redirected to standard input: compression is
then detected automatically, and a contradicting option is ignored (with
a warning when the input is standard input).
Pipes other than standard input, such as shell process substitutions and
named FIFOs, are always read as uncompressed data; compressed data must
arrive on standard input or as a named file.
.TP
\f[V]--fasta_width\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Set the maximal width of sequences when writing fasta files.
Longer sequences are folded and written on several lines.
Default width is 80 nucleotides.
Set to zero (0) to suppress folding.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_ascii\f[R] 33|64
Specify the \f[I]offset\f[R] used as the basis for the fastq quality
score when reading fastq files.
For example, an offset of 33 means that a quality value of 41 is
represented by the 74th ASCII symbol (33 + 41 = 74), which is `J'.
See \f[V]ascii(7)\f[R] for a view of the ASCII character set.
The offset value is either 33 or 64, default is 33.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_qmax\f[R] \f[I]integer\f[R]
Specify the maximal quality score accepted when reading fastq sequences.
Stop with an error message if a quality score higher than the specified
value is read.
The offset (see \f[V]--fastq_ascii\f[R]) plus the maximal score may not
exceed 126, the last printable ASCII character: scores up to 93 with
offset 33, up to 62 with offset 64.
The default is 41, which is usual for recent Sanger/Illumina 1.8+ files.
.TP
\f[V]--fastq_qmin\f[R] \f[I]integer\f[R]
Specify the minimal quality score accepted when reading fastq sequences.
Stop with an error message if a quality score lower than the specified
value is read.
The offset (see \f[V]--fastq_ascii\f[R]) plus the minimal score must be
at least 33, the first printable ASCII character: scores down to 0 with
offset 33, down to -31 with offset 64.
The value may therefore be negative, which is what the negative scores
of older formats require, but note that those formats are not supported
(see \f[V]vsearch-fastq(5)\f[R]).
The default is 0, which is usual for recent Sanger/Illumina 1.8+ files.
.TP
\f[V]--gzip_decompress\f[R]
Specify that the \f[I]input pipe\f[R] is streaming data compressed using
Lempel-Ziv coding.
See \f[V]gzip(1)\f[R] for more details.
This option is required when compressed data arrives on standard input
through a pipe (`-'), where the format cannot be detected without
consuming the stream.
It is not needed when reading from a regular file compressed with gzip,
nor when such a file is redirected to standard input: compression is
then detected automatically, and a contradicting option is ignored (with
a warning when the input is standard input).
Pipes other than standard input, such as shell process substitutions and
named FIFOs, are always read as uncompressed data; compressed data must
arrive on standard input or as a named file.
.TP
\f[V]--label_suffix\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Add the suffix \f[I]string\f[R] to sequence headers when writing fasta
or fastq files.
For example, with \f[V]--label_suffix \[dq];status=healthy\[dq]\f[R],
sequence header `>seq1' becomes `>seq1;status=healthy'.
.TP
\f[V]--lengthout\f[R]
Add a sequence length annotation (\f[V];length=integer\f[R]) to each
sequence header when writing fasta or fastq files.
.TP
\f[V]--log\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write messages to \f[I]filename\f[R].
Messages include program version, start and finish times, elapsed time,
amount of memory available, maximum amount of memory consumed, number of
cores and command line options, and if need be, command-specific
informational messages, warnings, and errors.
.TP
\f[V]--no_progress\f[R]
Suppress the gradually increasing progress indicator normally written to
the \f[I]standard error\f[R] \f[V]stderr(3)\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--quiet\f[R]
Suppress messages to the \f[I]standard output\f[R] \f[V]stdout(3)\f[R]
and \f[I]standard error\f[R] \f[V]stderr(3)\f[R], except for warnings
and error messages.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Replace sequence headers with the prefix \f[I]string\f[R] and a ticker
(1, 2, 3, etc.).
For example, with \f[V]--relabel \[dq]cluster:\[dq]\f[R], the first
sequence header becomes `>cluster:1', the second sequence header becomes
`>cluster:2', and so on.
To retain annotations, use their corresponding options
(\f[V]--lengthout\f[R], \f[V]--eeout\f[R], and \f[V]--sizeout\f[R]).
Use \f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R] to also retain old sequence identifiers.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R]
Retain old sequence identifiers by including them at the end of the new
headers, after a space.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel_md5\f[R]
Replace each sequence header with the MD5 digest derived from the
sequence itself.
The sequence is converted to upper case, and each `U' is replaced with a
`T' before computation of the digest.
The MD5 digest is a 128-bit value (16 bytes), represented using a string
of 32 ASCII characters.
Each pair of characters encodes an hexadecimal value, ranging from
\f[V]x00\f[R] to \f[V]xff\f[R].
See \f[V]md5(3)\f[R] for more details, and \f[V]--relabel_sha1\f[R] for
an alternative hashing algorithm.
To retain annotations, use their corresponding options
(\f[V]--lengthout\f[R], \f[V]--eeout\f[R], and \f[V]--sizeout\f[R]).
Use \f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R] to also retain old sequence identifiers.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel_self\f[R]
Replace each sequence header with the sequence itself.
To retain annotations, use their corresponding options
(\f[V]--lengthout\f[R], \f[V]--eeout\f[R], and \f[V]--sizeout\f[R]).
Use \f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R] to also retain old sequence identifiers.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel_sha1\f[R]
Replace each sequence header with the SHA1 digest derived from the
sequence itself.
The sequence is converted to upper case, and each `U' is replaced with a
`T' before computation of the digest.
The SHA1 digest is a 160-bit value (20 bytes), represented using a
string of 40 ASCII characters.
Each pair of characters encodes an hexadecimal value, ranging from
\f[V]x00\f[R] to \f[V]xff\f[R].
See \f[V]sha1(3)\f[R] for more details, and \f[V]--relabel_md5\f[R] for
an alternative hashing algorithm.
To retain annotations, use their corresponding options
(\f[V]--lengthout\f[R], \f[V]--eeout\f[R], and \f[V]--sizeout\f[R]).
Use \f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R] to also retain old sequence identifiers.
.TP
\f[V]--sample\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Add the given sample identifier \f[I]string\f[R] to sequence headers
when writing fasta or fastq files.
For instance, if \f[I]string\f[R] is `ABC', the text
\f[V];sample=ABC\f[R] will be added to the headers.
\f[I]string\f[R] is silently truncated at the first `;' or whitespace
character (space, tab, newline, carriage return, vertical tab or form
feed), so such characters should not be used in \f[I]string\f[R].
Other characters (alphabetical, numerical and punctuations) are
accepted.
.TP
\f[V]--xee\f[R]
Strip expected error (ee) annotations from sequence headers when writing
fasta or fastq files.
Search for the pattern \f[V][>\[at];]ee=float[;]\f[R].
Expected error annotations are added by the synonymous options
\f[V]--fastq_eeout\f[R] and \f[V]--eeout\f[R] described in
\f[V]vsearch-fastx_filter(1)\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--xlength\f[R]
Strip sequence length annotations from sequence headers when writing
fasta or fastq files.
Search for the pattern \f[V][>\[at];]length=integer[;]\f[R].
Sequence length annotations are added by the \f[V]--lengthout\f[R]
option.
.TP
\f[V]--xsize\f[R]
Strip abundance annotations from sequence headers when writing fasta or
fastq files.
Search for the pattern \f[V][>\[at];]size=integer[;]\f[R].
Abundance annotations are added by the \f[V]--sizeout\f[R] option.
.SS ignored options
.TP
\f[V]--threads\f[R] \f[I]positive non-null integer\f[R]
Command is not multithreaded, option has no effect (a warning is printed
when more than one thread is requested).
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Filter a fastq file, discarding reads with an expected error above 1.0,
and write kept sequences in fasta format:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch \[rs]
    --fastq_filter input.fastq \[rs]
    --fastq_maxee 1.0 \[rs]
    --fastaout filtered.fasta
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Truncate reads to 250 bases, then discard those with an expected error
above 0.5, annotating output headers with the expected error:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch \[rs]
    --fastq_filter input.fastq \[rs]
    --fastq_trunclen 250 \[rs]
    --fastq_maxee 0.5 \[rs]
    --eeout \[rs]
    --fastqout filtered.fastq
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Strip a fixed-length primer from the left, then filter by minimum length
and maximum expected error rate:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch \[rs]
    --fastq_filter input.fastq \[rs]
    --fastq_stripleft 20 \[rs]
    --fastq_minlen 100 \[rs]
    --fastq_maxee_rate 0.01 \[rs]
    --fastqout trimmed.fastq \[rs]
    --fastqout_discarded discarded.fastq
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Filter paired-end reads, keeping only pairs where both reads pass:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch \[rs]
    --fastq_filter forward.fastq \[rs]
    --reverse reverse.fastq \[rs]
    --fastq_maxee 1.0 \[rs]
    --fastqout kept_fwd.fastq \[rs]
    --fastqout_rev kept_rev.fastq \[rs]
    --fastqout_discarded discarded_fwd.fastq \[rs]
    --fastqout_discarded_rev discarded_rev.fastq
\f[R]
.fi
